1
00:00:00,000 --> 00:00:03,518
Three everyday moments in China that aren't about being polite.

2
00:00:03,843 --> 00:00:05,027
Names first.

3
00:00:05,468 --> 00:00:09,311
In the US, using your friend's mom's first name sounds friendly.

4
00:00:09,590 --> 00:00:14,060
In many Chinese families, the name you choose maps the relationship.

5
00:00:14,443 --> 00:00:17,798
"Auntie" places you; a first name skips a step.

6
00:00:18,123 --> 00:00:20,282
At the door, the same logic applies.

7
00:00:20,526 --> 00:00:23,777
In the US, tearing the paper off a gift is fun.

8
00:00:24,102 --> 00:00:28,212
In many Chinese homes, the thank-you comes first; the gift can wait.

9
00:00:28,595 --> 00:00:29,849
It's not about the gift.

10
00:00:30,058 --> 00:00:31,300
It's about the person.

11
00:00:31,625 --> 00:00:33,448
At the table, it changes again.

12
00:00:33,773 --> 00:00:36,443
In the US, a tip is part of the service.

13
00:00:36,687 --> 00:00:39,752
In China, the menu price is usually the whole price.

14
00:00:40,193 --> 00:00:43,909
Extra can feel less like generosity — more like charity.

15
00:00:44,234 --> 00:00:45,186
Different rules.

16
00:00:45,395 --> 00:00:47,310
Same instinct: respect.

17
00:00:47,752 --> 00:00:49,458
Which one would you have gotten wrong?

